[{:db/id 17592186231405 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-784a3ecb-16b0-5fa6-bed2-991dfa6c8b60"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186391878 :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-1,1]$, where $f$ is $2$-periodic and odd. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-784a3ecb-16b0-5fa6-bed2-991dfa6c8b60" :question/id #uuid "784a3ecb-16b0-5fa6-bed2-991dfa6c8b60" :question/math-academy-id "q-344269" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-6,6]$, where $f$ is $12$-periodic and odd. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $12$-periodic on $[-6,6]$, we have $L=6$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis \n\n$$\ncos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6})\\,\\text{and}\\,sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6}).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is odd, its Fourier series contains only sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a sine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6})\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-51585168-5999-52ac-a302-915344f4951e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-51585168-5999-52ac-a302-915344f4951e" :answer-field/id #uuid "51585168-5999-52ac-a302-915344f4951e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-8eef8445-476b-5036-b260-03f7d53b7715" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4c7a31b2-8edf-5725-b08a-4ca04a19f52d" "answer-00db171e-86f4-578f-baa9-eeb4a13a294d" "answer-8cc33023-61a9-57c1-b84d-42d2debd3175" "answer-8eef8445-476b-5036-b260-03f7d53b7715"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c7a31b2-8edf-5725-b08a-4ca04a19f52d" :answer/id #uuid "4c7a31b2-8edf-5725-b08a-4ca04a19f52d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00db171e-86f4-578f-baa9-eeb4a13a294d" :answer/id #uuid "00db171e-86f4-578f-baa9-eeb4a13a294d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{12})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8cc33023-61a9-57c1-b84d-42d2debd3175" :answer/id #uuid "8cc33023-61a9-57c1-b84d-42d2debd3175" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{12})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8eef8445-476b-5036-b260-03f7d53b7715" :answer/id #uuid "8eef8445-476b-5036-b260-03f7d53b7715" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-6ade1ba4-a844-5f17-ae51-ad9fb452b45a" :activity/id #uuid "6ade1ba4-a844-5f17-ae51-ad9fb452b45a" :activity/title "Solving BVPs Using Fourier Series" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050814 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-3aab5294-b9a1-5c24-942b-52709d4fcd37" "step-b678c6bd-3133-5ec1-bed3-d0d9d93c8e3c" "step-37c6243c-06ec-5738-995f-c4f49f7b7696" "step-2b173ba2-96ab-523d-bf33-e069587b7119" "step-8362d1b1-2815-579d-a7ce-8b445abb4156"] :activity/first-step "step-3aab5294-b9a1-5c24-942b-52709d4fcd37" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-3aab5294-b9a1-5c24-942b-52709d4fcd37" :step/id #uuid "3aab5294-b9a1-5c24-942b-52709d4fcd37" :step/content 17592186455474 :step/next "step-b678c6bd-3133-5ec1-bed3-d0d9d93c8e3c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b678c6bd-3133-5ec1-bed3-d0d9d93c8e3c" :step/id #uuid "b678c6bd-3133-5ec1-bed3-d0d9d93c8e3c" :step/content 17592186391878 :step/next "step-37c6243c-06ec-5738-995f-c4f49f7b7696" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-37c6243c-06ec-5738-995f-c4f49f7b7696" :step/id #uuid "37c6243c-06ec-5738-995f-c4f49f7b7696" :step/content 17592186372860 :step/next "step-2b173ba2-96ab-523d-bf33-e069587b7119" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2b173ba2-96ab-523d-bf33-e069587b7119" :step/id #uuid "2b173ba2-96ab-523d-bf33-e069587b7119" :step/content "question-784a3ecb-16b0-5fa6-bed2-991dfa6c8b60" :step/next "step-8362d1b1-2815-579d-a7ce-8b445abb4156" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8362d1b1-2815-579d-a7ce-8b445abb4156" :step/id #uuid "8362d1b1-2815-579d-a7ce-8b445abb4156" :step/content 17592186385296 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186184192 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b3fa1c78-fc26-501e-be9d-6a19d88c4385"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-b3fa1c78-fc26-501e-be9d-6a19d88c4385" :question/id #uuid "b3fa1c78-fc26-501e-be9d-6a19d88c4385" :question/math-academy-id "q-112614" :question/problem "The equation $-2{x}^{2}-16x-23=0$ can be written in the form $2(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-2{x}^{2}-16x-23) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+16x+23 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+16x+23 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+16x & =-23 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}+8x) & =-23 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4\\cdot x+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2}] & =-23 \\\\ 2[{(x+4)}^{2}-16] & =-23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x+4)}^{2}-2\\cdot 16 & =-23 \\\\ 2{(x+4)}^{2}-32 & =-23 \\\\ 2{(x+4)}^{2} & =-23+32 \\\\ 2{(x+4)}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=9.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x+4)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(x+4)}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x+4)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{2}} \\\\ |x+4| & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ x+4 & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ x & =-4\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-53de4293-1518-51e6-b385-eaf97b7abfbb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-53de4293-1518-51e6-b385-eaf97b7abfbb" :answer-field/id #uuid "53de4293-1518-51e6-b385-eaf97b7abfbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-02ea1683-3673-5e35-9072-ff3ec43edd3d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0b8dd15f-6ef6-5465-ba84-3a0eaa094c6a" "answer-b903bd14-e04d-5fe0-bd6c-e730be650761" "answer-c3cfb96e-8f1f-5a2a-8263-292cf5833e90" "answer-02ea1683-3673-5e35-9072-ff3ec43edd3d" "answer-d197f0c1-c269-5113-87f5-a4378c002223"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b8dd15f-6ef6-5465-ba84-3a0eaa094c6a" :answer/id #uuid "0b8dd15f-6ef6-5465-ba84-3a0eaa094c6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "q=4,\\,x=-1\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b903bd14-e04d-5fe0-bd6c-e730be650761" :answer/id #uuid "b903bd14-e04d-5fe0-bd6c-e730be650761" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "q=4,\\,x=-1\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3cfb96e-8f1f-5a2a-8263-292cf5833e90" :answer/id #uuid "c3cfb96e-8f1f-5a2a-8263-292cf5833e90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "q=9,\\,x=-2\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02ea1683-3673-5e35-9072-ff3ec43edd3d" :answer/id #uuid "02ea1683-3673-5e35-9072-ff3ec43edd3d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "q=9,\\,x=-4\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d197f0c1-c269-5113-87f5-a4378c002223" :answer/id #uuid "d197f0c1-c269-5113-87f5-a4378c002223" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "q=9,\\,x=-6\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-6f2c220d-dea6-5085-bfd4-266f1b303d4e" :activity/id #uuid "6f2c220d-dea6-5085-bfd4-266f1b303d4e" :activity/title "Solving Quadratic Equations With Leading Coefficients by Completing the Square" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050222 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-1620937e-880d-5b09-853a-0f9758011091" "step-b1ef3657-c6c8-5b89-902f-79ba06b73e9d" "step-52da9c0a-3535-5459-a1f4-e582ca345e3b" "step-4206b113-f5c1-5c37-935a-359976937b39" "step-1440d411-5028-5239-a5b0-d36102654409"] :activity/first-step "step-1620937e-880d-5b09-853a-0f9758011091" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-1620937e-880d-5b09-853a-0f9758011091" :step/id #uuid "1620937e-880d-5b09-853a-0f9758011091" :step/content 17592186265780 :step/next "step-b1ef3657-c6c8-5b89-902f-79ba06b73e9d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b1ef3657-c6c8-5b89-902f-79ba06b73e9d" :step/id #uuid "b1ef3657-c6c8-5b89-902f-79ba06b73e9d" :step/content 17592186332964 :step/next "step-52da9c0a-3535-5459-a1f4-e582ca345e3b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-52da9c0a-3535-5459-a1f4-e582ca345e3b" :step/id #uuid "52da9c0a-3535-5459-a1f4-e582ca345e3b" :step/content 17592186404336 :step/next "step-4206b113-f5c1-5c37-935a-359976937b39" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-4206b113-f5c1-5c37-935a-359976937b39" :step/id #uuid "4206b113-f5c1-5c37-935a-359976937b39" :step/content 17592186247291 :step/next "step-1440d411-5028-5239-a5b0-d36102654409" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1440d411-5028-5239-a5b0-d36102654409" :step/id #uuid "1440d411-5028-5239-a5b0-d36102654409" :step/content "question-b3fa1c78-fc26-501e-be9d-6a19d88c4385" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073912 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3223d2f4-ea53-55be-b26b-e4775a16b327" "question-6c6e7fd6-6ef2-5487-99ae-05f731828948"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073598 :question/worked-solution "Given two similar polygons, the similarity ratio is the ratio of any two corresponding sides. \n\nNotice that, for example, $\\overline{LM}$ is the longest side of $NLMP.$ Hence, it corresponds to the longest side of $ABCD,$ which is  $\\overline{BC}.$ \n\nTherefore, the similarity ratio is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{BC}{LM} \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{22} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The ratio of the smaller polygon to the larger polygon would be $\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)}=\\dfrac{2}{3}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073913 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8c8cab05-4b0d-578a-9cd0-38ffa882719a" "question-8747f1cc-7c01-5370-9ad7-927463f6175f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073599 :question/problem "Which of the following quadrilaterals are similar to $\\mathcal{R}$ (other than $\\mathcal R$ itself)? \n\n![](images/5a/5a7c9208a16a639ef6b08855a6caaacb39c121dd4ccf45271d98fcc70f1a8fed.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $\\mathcal{R}$ is a rectangle, meaning all the angles measure $90^\\circ,$ and it has $2$ pairs of parallel congruent sides.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given polygons.\n\n- $\\mathcal{Q}$ is *not* similar to $\\mathcal{R}.$ The polygon $\\mathcal{Q}$ is a rectangle, and the corresponding angles are congruent, but the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are not the same: \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{6}\\neq\\dfrac{3}{3}\n$$\n\n- $\\mathcal{P}$ is similar to $\\mathcal{R}.$ Indeed, $\\mathcal{P}$ is a rectangle. Furthermore, the corresponding angles are congruent, and the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are the same:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{6}=\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\n- $\\mathcal{S}$ is *not* similar to $\\mathcal{R}$ because $\\mathcal{S}$ is not a rectangle (it's a parallelogram).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathcal{P}$ only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073914 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e7e53512-d30f-5e5f-8560-8aa96c633833" "question-f26dd26e-832a-57e0-87b3-fbf105b066fa" "question-20014b11-37e4-5374-8b1b-750de6a641aa" "question-7546430f-d626-5e00-b8d1-dad9719eee9d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073600 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the measures of unknown angles. Since the sum of the measures of internal angles of a quadrilateral is $360^\\circ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C+m∠D & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {55}^{∘}+m∠B+{90}^{∘}+{125}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B+{270}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠K+m∠L+m∠M+m∠N & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {55}^{∘}+{90}^{∘}+m∠M+{125}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠M+{270}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠M & ={90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the quadrilaterals are similar, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠A≅∠K,\\,∠D≅∠N,\\,∠C≅∠L≅∠B≅∠M.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo far, we have that \n\n- $A$ corresponds to $K,$ and\n\n- $D$ corresponds to $N.$\n\nFrom the angle measurements, it's unclear whether $C$ corresponds to $L$ or $M$ (these are all right angles). However, $C$ sits between a $125^\\circ$ angle and a $90^\\circ$ angle, and so does $M$ (whereas $L$ does not). So, we also have that \n\n- $C$ corresponds to $M,$ and\n\n- $B$ corresponds to $L.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that a correct similarity statement is \n\n$$\nABCD \\sim  KLMN.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073915 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8d0a74cc-718a-5740-8268-b7272012d842" "question-11d089d5-e1bc-5fc5-afc2-069d5d90d5e1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073601 :question/problem "Given that $ABCD \\sim A'B'C'D',$ $AB = 3\\,\\mathrm{ft},$ and $BC = 4\\,\\mathrm{ft},$\nwhich of the following could be the values of $A'B'$ and $B'C'?$\n\nI. ${A'B'} = 15\\, \\mathrm{ft}, \\, {B'C'} = 20\\, \\mathrm{ft}$\n\nII. ${A'B'} = 30\\, \\mathrm{ft}, \\, {B'C'} = 44\\, \\mathrm{ft}$\n\nIII. ${A'B'} = 18\\, \\mathrm{ft}, \\, {B'C'} = 24\\, \\mathrm{ft}$" :question/worked-solution "For similar polygons, the ratios of lengths of the corresponding sides should be the same.\n\n- For option I, the ratios are the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{AB}{{A}^{′}{B}^{′}} & =\\frac{3}{15}=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{BC}{{B}^{′}{C}^{′}} & =\\frac{4}{20}=\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option I indeed gives possible values of $A'B'$ and $B'C'.$\n\n- For option II, the ratios are different:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{AB}{{A}^{′}{B}^{′}} & =\\frac{3}{30}=\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ \\frac{BC}{{B}^{′}{C}^{′}} & =\\frac{4}{44}=\\frac{1}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option II does not give possible values of $A'B'$ and $B'C'.$\n\n- For option III, the ratios are the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{AB}{{A}^{′}{B}^{′}} & =\\frac{3}{18}=\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{BC}{{B}^{′}{C}^{′}} & =\\frac{4}{24}=\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option III indeed gives possible values of $A'B'$ and $B'C'.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-3223d2f4-ea53-55be-b26b-e4775a16b327" :question/id #uuid "3223d2f4-ea53-55be-b26b-e4775a16b327" :question/math-academy-id "q-87955" :question/problem "![](images/83/83881bdbd7df1527338db3de5e88b69003b7d30c8c25d4583127b1579a28cf83.png)\n\nGiven that $△DEF∼△PQR$ (picture is not to scale), find the similarity ratio.\n\n*Hint: Consider the ratio of the **larger** polygon to the **smaller** polygon.*" :question/worked-solution "The similarity ratio is the ratio of the corresponding sides.\n\nSince $△DEF∼△PQR,$ the side $\\overset{―}{DE}$ corresponds to the side $\\overset{―}{PQ}$\n\nTherefore, the similarity ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{PQ}{DE} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-825ac6b4-8e09-54a8-93d8-364062e99d83"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-825ac6b4-8e09-54a8-93d8-364062e99d83" :answer-field/id #uuid "825ac6b4-8e09-54a8-93d8-364062e99d83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-26f8df91-a004-5ed4-a842-1ee3477c18d3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-81fe1089-50b4-5826-addb-12509edadade" "answer-501fee38-c464-5aac-b956-c55ca47fbb99" "answer-26f8df91-a004-5ed4-a842-1ee3477c18d3" "answer-bb3ec2c0-e6c3-5717-9355-9c66c24d12d9" "answer-1be58a25-a177-5a2e-b607-0fc07b39bf72"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81fe1089-50b4-5826-addb-12509edadade" :answer/id #uuid "81fe1089-50b4-5826-addb-12509edadade" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-501fee38-c464-5aac-b956-c55ca47fbb99" :answer/id #uuid "501fee38-c464-5aac-b956-c55ca47fbb99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26f8df91-a004-5ed4-a842-1ee3477c18d3" :answer/id #uuid "26f8df91-a004-5ed4-a842-1ee3477c18d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb3ec2c0-e6c3-5717-9355-9c66c24d12d9" :answer/id #uuid "bb3ec2c0-e6c3-5717-9355-9c66c24d12d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1be58a25-a177-5a2e-b607-0fc07b39bf72" :answer/id #uuid "1be58a25-a177-5a2e-b607-0fc07b39bf72" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6c6e7fd6-6ef2-5487-99ae-05f731828948" :question/id #uuid "6c6e7fd6-6ef2-5487-99ae-05f731828948" :question/math-academy-id "q-121032" :question/problem "![](images/39/392309f189b8840862795d3b111c20956ec2366b349c6a60a6b0288052d451c2.png)\n\nGiven that $△ABC∼△VTU$ (picture is not to scale), find the similarity ratio.\n\n*Hint: Consider the ratio of the **smaller** polygon to the **larger** polygon.*" :question/worked-solution "The similarity ratio is the ratio of the corresponding sides.\n\nSince $△ABC∼△VTU,$ the side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ corresponds to the side $\\overset{―}{TU}$\n\nTherefore, the similarity ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{BC}{TU} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-25001951-6cdd-5c9e-8efc-d2bd29e9bd9e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-25001951-6cdd-5c9e-8efc-d2bd29e9bd9e" :answer-field/id #uuid "25001951-6cdd-5c9e-8efc-d2bd29e9bd9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d07eb44e-094c-5aa7-a98b-5379e72cbf6f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a21206bd-ef41-5608-b022-b7d66d665a11" "answer-16f70ab3-d1fd-5534-ae2c-bd54b0d418dd" "answer-d07eb44e-094c-5aa7-a98b-5379e72cbf6f" "answer-2bf243e1-daf6-5dbe-8ce6-ed87a3099ae5" "answer-6caa123f-3987-50a3-bd49-4971d88b75b1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a21206bd-ef41-5608-b022-b7d66d665a11" :answer/id #uuid "a21206bd-ef41-5608-b022-b7d66d665a11" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16f70ab3-d1fd-5534-ae2c-bd54b0d418dd" :answer/id #uuid "16f70ab3-d1fd-5534-ae2c-bd54b0d418dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d07eb44e-094c-5aa7-a98b-5379e72cbf6f" :answer/id #uuid "d07eb44e-094c-5aa7-a98b-5379e72cbf6f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2bf243e1-daf6-5dbe-8ce6-ed87a3099ae5" :answer/id #uuid "2bf243e1-daf6-5dbe-8ce6-ed87a3099ae5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6caa123f-3987-50a3-bd49-4971d88b75b1" :answer/id #uuid "6caa123f-3987-50a3-bd49-4971d88b75b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8c8cab05-4b0d-578a-9cd0-38ffa882719a" :question/id #uuid "8c8cab05-4b0d-578a-9cd0-38ffa882719a" :question/math-academy-id "q-87971" :question/problem "![](images/1a/1ad27414fe6da6009a54815aff3137365067218e5b73f62e34ef6d3ae2527058.png)\n\nWhich of the above quadrilaterals are similar to $T$ (other than $T$ itself)?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $T$ is a trapezoid with one pair of parallel opposite sides. Since the angles adjacent to the bases are congruent, it is an isosceles trapezoid. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given polygons.\n\n- $S$ is *not* similar to $T.$ The polygon $S$ is an isosceles trapezoid, and the corresponding angles are congruent, but the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are not the same: \n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{16}\\ne \\frac{4}{4}\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* similar to $T$ because $Q$ is a right trapezoid since it has right angles.\n\n- $P$ is similar to $T.$ Indeed, $P$ is an isosceles trapezoid. Furthermore, the corresponding angles are congruent, and the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{16}=\\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d26a1b39-5d9a-522e-b93e-64f3b7de831a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d26a1b39-5d9a-522e-b93e-64f3b7de831a" :answer-field/id #uuid "d26a1b39-5d9a-522e-b93e-64f3b7de831a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-54ee511f-911a-559f-8bfb-87b06f994f55" :answer-field/choices ["answer-465109ee-90d8-50db-9713-4cfb3cbb5d12" "answer-54ee511f-911a-559f-8bfb-87b06f994f55" "answer-4fb24f3c-6f2d-5ebe-a9bf-1a71a8cb0f3b" "answer-bdee3bc0-bc14-5eac-af24-cf0cdd490fbb" "answer-10fbcd74-c10f-55e8-ac05-f01fea77a1e5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-465109ee-90d8-50db-9713-4cfb3cbb5d12" :answer/id #uuid "465109ee-90d8-50db-9713-4cfb3cbb5d12" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54ee511f-911a-559f-8bfb-87b06f994f55" :answer/id #uuid "54ee511f-911a-559f-8bfb-87b06f994f55" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4fb24f3c-6f2d-5ebe-a9bf-1a71a8cb0f3b" :answer/id #uuid "4fb24f3c-6f2d-5ebe-a9bf-1a71a8cb0f3b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Q$ and $S$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bdee3bc0-bc14-5eac-af24-cf0cdd490fbb" :answer/id #uuid "bdee3bc0-bc14-5eac-af24-cf0cdd490fbb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10fbcd74-c10f-55e8-ac05-f01fea77a1e5" :answer/id #uuid "10fbcd74-c10f-55e8-ac05-f01fea77a1e5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8747f1cc-7c01-5370-9ad7-927463f6175f" :question/id #uuid "8747f1cc-7c01-5370-9ad7-927463f6175f" :question/math-academy-id "q-121036" :question/problem "![](images/e4/e4a79a8b0eb2c0c79b1cdcb0623dc913169db8a46aab9b0a18c23f96ac61a0cd.png)\n\nWhich of the above quadrilaterals are similar to $P$ (other than $P$ itself)?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $P$ is a kite with a pair of opposite congruent angles and $2$ pairs of congruent sides. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given polygons.\n\n- $R$ is *not* similar to $P$ because $R$ is not a kite.\n\n- $S$ is similar to $P.$ Indeed, $S$ is a kite. Furthermore, the corresponding angles are congruent, and the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2.5}=\\frac{6}{5}\n$$\n\n- $T$ is similar to $P.$ Indeed, $T$ is a kite. Furthermore, the corresponding angles are congruent, and the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2.5}{2.5}=\\frac{5}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$S$ and $T$ only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d2b35f2c-efc3-59cb-b3ac-4a86eb509d98"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d2b35f2c-efc3-59cb-b3ac-4a86eb509d98" :answer-field/id #uuid "d2b35f2c-efc3-59cb-b3ac-4a86eb509d98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-68e30e74-d0a8-5bc3-8434-a609f8e1e5a9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0dc0b8b2-179d-59ee-be74-60876929c3e7" "answer-76601693-bc59-54d3-acb6-5642e2b10ded" "answer-68e30e74-d0a8-5bc3-8434-a609f8e1e5a9" "answer-3ecc9ee7-1c24-578d-8c59-028a37ce7c4a" "answer-7410a378-e6fc-5722-9555-785db18e4f2e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0dc0b8b2-179d-59ee-be74-60876929c3e7" :answer/id #uuid "0dc0b8b2-179d-59ee-be74-60876929c3e7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76601693-bc59-54d3-acb6-5642e2b10ded" :answer/id #uuid "76601693-bc59-54d3-acb6-5642e2b10ded" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$R,$ $S,$ and $T$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68e30e74-d0a8-5bc3-8434-a609f8e1e5a9" :answer/id #uuid "68e30e74-d0a8-5bc3-8434-a609f8e1e5a9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$S$ and $T$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ecc9ee7-1c24-578d-8c59-028a37ce7c4a" :answer/id #uuid "3ecc9ee7-1c24-578d-8c59-028a37ce7c4a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7410a378-e6fc-5722-9555-785db18e4f2e" :answer/id #uuid "7410a378-e6fc-5722-9555-785db18e4f2e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e7e53512-d30f-5e5f-8560-8aa96c633833" :question/id #uuid "e7e53512-d30f-5e5f-8560-8aa96c633833" :question/math-academy-id "q-121176" :question/problem "![](images/14/145db132983ccf795c8fc466cd6e1ce77b7a33a3119fb281c3592ad5234cb08a.png)\n\nThe two quadrilaterals shown above are similar. What is the correct similarity statement?" :question/worked-solution "Since, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠A≅∠P,\\,∠B≅∠S,\\,∠C≅∠R,\\,∠D≅∠Q,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n- $A$ corresponds to $P,$\n\n- $B$ corresponds to $S,$\n\n- $C$ corresponds to $R,$\n\n- $D$ corresponds to $Q.$\n\nTherefore, $ABCD∼PSRQ.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-55a75ccf-e4a8-5bd8-baa7-adb30b3b66e1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-55a75ccf-e4a8-5bd8-baa7-adb30b3b66e1" :answer-field/id #uuid "55a75ccf-e4a8-5bd8-baa7-adb30b3b66e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3f09aaa3-9879-538a-af7e-51e054b41b6a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-44d05074-7ea7-5102-8bc6-2cdae02f2abf" "answer-3f09aaa3-9879-538a-af7e-51e054b41b6a" "answer-74a301d6-1665-5c48-9799-21482fc5cf59" "answer-265bb299-b7de-584b-aa4d-8a6fb58c6501" "answer-955defbe-ed2d-5d17-9905-b1f52f78332b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44d05074-7ea7-5102-8bc6-2cdae02f2abf" :answer/id #uuid "44d05074-7ea7-5102-8bc6-2cdae02f2abf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "ABCD∼PQRS" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f09aaa3-9879-538a-af7e-51e054b41b6a" :answer/id #uuid "3f09aaa3-9879-538a-af7e-51e054b41b6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "ABCD∼PSRQ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74a301d6-1665-5c48-9799-21482fc5cf59" :answer/id #uuid "74a301d6-1665-5c48-9799-21482fc5cf59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "ABCD∼RQPS" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-265bb299-b7de-584b-aa4d-8a6fb58c6501" :answer/id #uuid "265bb299-b7de-584b-aa4d-8a6fb58c6501" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "ABCD∼RSPQ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-955defbe-ed2d-5d17-9905-b1f52f78332b" :answer/id #uuid "955defbe-ed2d-5d17-9905-b1f52f78332b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "ABCD∼SRQP" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f26dd26e-832a-57e0-87b3-fbf105b066fa" :question/id #uuid "f26dd26e-832a-57e0-87b3-fbf105b066fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-53576" :question/problem "![](images/e9/e9fa701b3efb9251e5591c40fe8c5011a73cd2b7794f6ce356860b2eeb1d8001.png)\n\nThe two quadrilaterals shown above are similar. What is the correct similarity statement?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the measures of unknown angles. Since the sum of the measures of internal angles of a quadrilateral is ${360}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C+m∠D & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {68}^{∘}+m∠B+{85}^{∘}+{111}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B+{264}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={96}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠M+m∠N+m∠K+m∠L & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {68}^{∘}+{96}^{∘}+m∠K+{111}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠K+{275}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠K & ={85}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the quadrilaterals are similar, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠A≅∠M,\\,∠B≅∠N,\\,∠C≅∠K,\\,∠D≅∠L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that \n\n- $A$ corresponds to $M,$\n\n- $B$ corresponds to $N,$\n\n- $C$ corresponds to $K,$\n\n- $D$ corresponds to $L.$\n\nTherefore, $ABCD∼MNKL.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a2d05394-8b11-58dd-b0a5-7d79939aa2f3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a2d05394-8b11-58dd-b0a5-7d79939aa2f3" :answer-field/id #uuid "a2d05394-8b11-58dd-b0a5-7d79939aa2f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-06963455-0bb5-572c-9fdf-d38c673c43f6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dee85f43-b139-5afb-a292-046a5c998f5a" "answer-ca01af76-b506-5ac2-b2ff-bc9d7b8a929e" "answer-06963455-0bb5-572c-9fdf-d38c673c43f6" "answer-038ab6e6-010a-58ad-b015-3510a7068a48" "answer-43b433bb-0495-5ef8-a9c8-bd3769eb7b81"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dee85f43-b139-5afb-a292-046a5c998f5a" :answer/id #uuid "dee85f43-b139-5afb-a292-046a5c998f5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "ABCD∼LKNM" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca01af76-b506-5ac2-b2ff-bc9d7b8a929e" :answer/id #uuid "ca01af76-b506-5ac2-b2ff-bc9d7b8a929e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "ABCD∼MLKN" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06963455-0bb5-572c-9fdf-d38c673c43f6" :answer/id #uuid "06963455-0bb5-572c-9fdf-d38c673c43f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "ABCD∼MNKL" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-038ab6e6-010a-58ad-b015-3510a7068a48" :answer/id #uuid "038ab6e6-010a-58ad-b015-3510a7068a48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "ABCD∼NKLM" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43b433bb-0495-5ef8-a9c8-bd3769eb7b81" :answer/id #uuid "43b433bb-0495-5ef8-a9c8-bd3769eb7b81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "ABCD∼NMLK" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-20014b11-37e4-5374-8b1b-750de6a641aa" :question/id #uuid "20014b11-37e4-5374-8b1b-750de6a641aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-88008" :question/problem "![](images/e7/e7c6cb8f0d7746c1d923ed80a9e05ba4f8eeb9ea3ae88c7b08bcd02037d8ec8a.png)\n\nThe two triangles shown above are similar. What is the correct similarity statement?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the measures of unknown angles. Since the sum of the measures of internal angles of a triangle is ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠H+m∠J+m∠L & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠H+{69}^{∘}+{40}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠H+{109}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠H & ={71}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠V+m∠W+m∠Y & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {71}^{∘}+m∠W+{40}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠W+{111}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠W & ={69}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the triangles are similar, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠H≅∠V,\\,∠J≅∠W,\\,∠L≅∠Y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that \n\n- $H$ corresponds to $V,$\n\n- $J$ corresponds to $W,$\n\n- $L$ corresponds to $Y.$\n\nTherefore, $HJL∼VWY.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f127219a-5ce9-57cb-92d2-db82d4cf9049"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f127219a-5ce9-57cb-92d2-db82d4cf9049" :answer-field/id #uuid "f127219a-5ce9-57cb-92d2-db82d4cf9049" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-134b5911-296f-5829-9e61-d54ff2ee3c06" :answer-field/choices ["answer-134b5911-296f-5829-9e61-d54ff2ee3c06" "answer-5a739a57-f8a7-57f6-9f07-9369498af250" "answer-359c7da6-462f-5ab6-b282-f7e995d4304b" "answer-2dd819f4-acd1-5080-bf8c-438dd9f0eef2" "answer-b2a62020-643f-54eb-a449-fe33ee4964ee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-134b5911-296f-5829-9e61-d54ff2ee3c06" :answer/id #uuid "134b5911-296f-5829-9e61-d54ff2ee3c06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "HJL∼VWY" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a739a57-f8a7-57f6-9f07-9369498af250" :answer/id #uuid "5a739a57-f8a7-57f6-9f07-9369498af250" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "HJL∼VYW" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-359c7da6-462f-5ab6-b282-f7e995d4304b" :answer/id #uuid "359c7da6-462f-5ab6-b282-f7e995d4304b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "HJL∼WYV" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2dd819f4-acd1-5080-bf8c-438dd9f0eef2" :answer/id #uuid "2dd819f4-acd1-5080-bf8c-438dd9f0eef2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "HJL∼YVW" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2a62020-643f-54eb-a449-fe33ee4964ee" :answer/id #uuid "b2a62020-643f-54eb-a449-fe33ee4964ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "HJL∼YWV" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7546430f-d626-5e00-b8d1-dad9719eee9d" :question/id #uuid "7546430f-d626-5e00-b8d1-dad9719eee9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-88010" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fca4f1d297c465ecf9da9bef652f1602edb6e6cd8887f6b3b8a83441a40871b.png)\n\nThe two quadrilaterals shown above are similar. What is the correct similarity statement?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the measures of unknown angles. Since the sum of the measures of internal angles of a quadrilateral is ${360}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠R+m∠T+m∠U+m∠V & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠R+{130}^{∘}+{50}^{∘}+{110}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠R+{290}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠R & ={70}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠O+m∠N+m∠Q+m∠P & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {70}^{∘}+{130}^{∘}+m∠Q+{110}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠Q+{310}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠Q & ={50}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the quadrilaterals are similar, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠R≅∠O,\\,∠T≅∠N,\\,∠U≅∠Q,\\,∠V≅∠P.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that \n\n- $R$ corresponds to $O,$\n\n- $T$ corresponds to $N,$\n\n- $U$ corresponds to $Q,$\n\n- $V$ corresponds to $P.$\n\nTherefore, $RTUV∼ONQP.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-74784b25-32a1-5400-b846-47a22b13397e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-74784b25-32a1-5400-b846-47a22b13397e" :answer-field/id #uuid "74784b25-32a1-5400-b846-47a22b13397e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5bbcb437-e4ac-5d10-a354-008c01636cf5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dce56259-996b-52e8-acfd-4e35a70c4345" "answer-4823d242-c49d-56b4-aff3-48822453e2bb" "answer-ae60cf2f-16ad-5289-8212-eb696f284bba" "answer-5bbcb437-e4ac-5d10-a354-008c01636cf5" "answer-b64145b0-6817-519c-8ee5-e35a44472707"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dce56259-996b-52e8-acfd-4e35a70c4345" :answer/id #uuid "dce56259-996b-52e8-acfd-4e35a70c4345" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "RTUV∼NOPQ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4823d242-c49d-56b4-aff3-48822453e2bb" :answer/id #uuid "4823d242-c49d-56b4-aff3-48822453e2bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "RTUV∼NOQP" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae60cf2f-16ad-5289-8212-eb696f284bba" :answer/id #uuid "ae60cf2f-16ad-5289-8212-eb696f284bba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "RTUV∼ONPQ" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5bbcb437-e4ac-5d10-a354-008c01636cf5" :answer/id #uuid "5bbcb437-e4ac-5d10-a354-008c01636cf5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "RTUV∼ONQP" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b64145b0-6817-519c-8ee5-e35a44472707" :answer/id #uuid "b64145b0-6817-519c-8ee5-e35a44472707" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "RTUV∼QPON" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8d0a74cc-718a-5740-8268-b7272012d842" :question/id #uuid "8d0a74cc-718a-5740-8268-b7272012d842" :question/math-academy-id "q-88034" :question/problem "Given that $△ABC∼△LMN,$ $BC=4\\,ft$ and $CA=2\\,ft,$\nwhich of the following could be the values of $MN$ and $NL?$\n\nI. $MN=2\\,ft,\\,NL=1\\,ft$\n\nII. $MN=12\\,ft,\\,NL=6\\,ft$\n\nIII. $MN=10\\,ft,\\,NL=8\\,ft$" :question/worked-solution "For similar polygons, the ratios of lengths of the corresponding sides should be the same.\n\n- Let's check option I:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{BC}{MN} & =\\frac{4}{2}=2, \\\\ \\frac{CA}{NL} & =\\frac{2}{1}=2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option I does give possible values of $MN$ and $NL.$\n\n- For option II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{BC}{MN} & =\\frac{4}{12}=\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ \\frac{CA}{NL} & =\\frac{2}{6}=\\frac{1}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option II does give possible values of $MN$ and $NL.$\n\n- For option III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{BC}{MN} & =\\frac{4}{10}=\\frac{2}{5}, \\\\ \\frac{CA}{NL} & =\\frac{2}{8}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option III does not give possible values of $MN$ and $NL.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6dbb8f80-3824-574a-98ef-fd79e58caa94"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6dbb8f80-3824-574a-98ef-fd79e58caa94" :answer-field/id #uuid "6dbb8f80-3824-574a-98ef-fd79e58caa94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e9277e82-8ab0-53f4-b029-196ab30ea2b0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e9277e82-8ab0-53f4-b029-196ab30ea2b0" "answer-6dd30f04-a917-5994-adcc-b8593c9f0de6" "answer-f5e74dec-c9c5-5c43-a3d2-8f466781a67f" "answer-6e38f0aa-f8eb-5da6-bbac-c3c3aa1785ee" "answer-6ae7b35a-6093-5cbe-8d7c-e232bbd15615"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9277e82-8ab0-53f4-b029-196ab30ea2b0" :answer/id #uuid "e9277e82-8ab0-53f4-b029-196ab30ea2b0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6dd30f04-a917-5994-adcc-b8593c9f0de6" :answer/id #uuid "6dd30f04-a917-5994-adcc-b8593c9f0de6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5e74dec-c9c5-5c43-a3d2-8f466781a67f" :answer/id #uuid "f5e74dec-c9c5-5c43-a3d2-8f466781a67f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e38f0aa-f8eb-5da6-bbac-c3c3aa1785ee" :answer/id #uuid "6e38f0aa-f8eb-5da6-bbac-c3c3aa1785ee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ae7b35a-6093-5cbe-8d7c-e232bbd15615" :answer/id #uuid "6ae7b35a-6093-5cbe-8d7c-e232bbd15615" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-11d089d5-e1bc-5fc5-afc2-069d5d90d5e1" :question/id #uuid "11d089d5-e1bc-5fc5-afc2-069d5d90d5e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-121053" :question/problem "Given that $ABCD∼LMNO,$ $CD=4\\,m$ and $AD=1\\,m,$\nwhich of the following could be the values of $NO$ and $LO?$\n\nI. $NO=12\\,m,\\,LO=3\\,m$\n\nII. $NO=16\\,m,\\,LO=5\\,m$\n\nIII. $NO=10\\,m,\\,LO=2\\,m$" :question/worked-solution "For similar polygons, the ratios of lengths of the corresponding sides should be the same.\n\n- Let's check for this in option I:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{CD}{NO} & =\\frac{4}{12}=\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ \\frac{AD}{LO} & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option I gives possible values of $NO$ and $LO.$\n\n- For option II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{CD}{NO} & =\\frac{4}{16}=\\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\frac{AD}{LO} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option III does *not* give possible values of $NO$ and $LO.$\n\n- For option III, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{CD}{NO} & =\\frac{4}{10}=\\frac{2}{5}, \\\\ \\frac{AD}{LO} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option III does *not* give possible values of $NO$ and $LO.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-535f178f-1bc4-52ea-8f27-866fded622bb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-535f178f-1bc4-52ea-8f27-866fded622bb" :answer-field/id #uuid "535f178f-1bc4-52ea-8f27-866fded622bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8d115afb-90af-52ac-9dba-3f7e51b99e2d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9dc8dd87-daad-5359-95f9-c362d3a2bcb7" "answer-8d115afb-90af-52ac-9dba-3f7e51b99e2d" "answer-26e4d84a-1733-5b6a-8de9-415d54c135e0" "answer-d5b4bc73-4915-5a93-8f69-3c7b84ab18ad" "answer-de2f5f31-5294-57d7-b388-91a3696fbb45"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9dc8dd87-daad-5359-95f9-c362d3a2bcb7" :answer/id #uuid "9dc8dd87-daad-5359-95f9-c362d3a2bcb7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d115afb-90af-52ac-9dba-3f7e51b99e2d" :answer/id #uuid "8d115afb-90af-52ac-9dba-3f7e51b99e2d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26e4d84a-1733-5b6a-8de9-415d54c135e0" :answer/id #uuid "26e4d84a-1733-5b6a-8de9-415d54c135e0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5b4bc73-4915-5a93-8f69-3c7b84ab18ad" :answer/id #uuid "d5b4bc73-4915-5a93-8f69-3c7b84ab18ad" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de2f5f31-5294-57d7-b388-91a3696fbb45" :answer/id #uuid "de2f5f31-5294-57d7-b388-91a3696fbb45" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186074983 :tutorial/content "Two polygons are **similar** if they have the same shape (though they may have different sizes). \n\nMore precisely, two polygons are similar if the following conditions hold:\n\n1\\. Their corresponding angles are congruent.\n\n2\\. The ratios of the lengths of their corresponding sides are all equal.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the triangles $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle EFG$ below.\n\n![](images/23/233fcb591a81d9ee6eef3caf1f4df0f0e959a18144177e89d5f0ac88c94525f4.png)\n\nFrom the figure above, we see that the corresponding angles are congruent:\n\n$$\n\\angle A \\cong \\angle E, \\qquad \\angle B \\cong \\angle F, \\qquad \\angle C \\cong \\angle G.\n$$\n\nWe also need to check whether the ratios of the corresponding sides are the same. That is, we have to check whether\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{AB}{EF} & =\\frac{BC}{FG}=\\frac{AC}{EG}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the lengths of the sides, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{AB}{EF} & =\\frac{6}{12}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{BC}{FG} & =\\frac{4}{8}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{AC}{EG} & =\\frac{5}{10}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe ratios are the same. Therefore, we conclude that the triangles $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle EFG$ are similar. The common ratio of the sides, $\\dfrac{1}{2},$ is called the **similarity ratio**.\n\nTo express mathematically that the triangles $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle EFG$ are similar, we write\n\n$$\n\\triangle ABC \\sim \\triangle EFG.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186075009 :tutorial/content "When writing a similarity statement, corresponding vertices should appear in the same position in the statement. For example, if we're given that \n\n$$\nABC \\sim PQR\n$$\n\nfor two polygons $ABC$ and $PQR,$ we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n\\angle A \\cong \\angle P, \\qquad \\angle B \\cong \\angle Q, \\qquad \\angle C \\cong \\angle R.\n$$\n\nWe can also deduce that\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{AB}{PQ}= \\dfrac{BC}{QR} =  \\dfrac{AC}{PR},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the similarity ratio." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073925 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186186378 :answer-field/choices 17592186186379] [:db/retract 17592186186378 :answer-field/choices 17592186186380] [:db/retract 17592186186378 :answer-field/choices 17592186186381] [:db/retract 17592186186378 :answer-field/choices 17592186186382] [:db/retract 17592186186378 :answer-field/choices 17592186186383] {:db/id 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9faef3f5-2412-5ae3-81dd-9f520c533b8b" "question-08394bc0-c2d2-5c3c-828d-b5a2969449ba"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188683 :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=100$ from a population where $60%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that between $55%$ and $65%$ of the sample have the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=100,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.6.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=100(0.6)=60>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=100(1-0.6)=40>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=100$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.6,\\frac{0.6(1-0.6)}{100}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.6,0.0024).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.55\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.65).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.55\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.65) & =P(\\frac{0.55-0.6}{\\sqrt{0.0024}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.65-0.6}{\\sqrt{0.0024}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.02\\le Z\\le 1.02) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.02)-P(Z\\le -1.02) \\\\  & =Φ(1.02)-Φ(-1.02)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186186378 :answer-field/correct "answer-cef03ff5-191a-5d70-bca6-cb830b7fda60" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ffaee5fa-f5c5-56f1-b2fb-18822e27e724" "answer-cd7d0474-7aea-50f9-ae3c-3081a543503a" "answer-cef03ff5-191a-5d70-bca6-cb830b7fda60" "answer-6092f662-75ed-5f8a-9564-15a7e43b742f" "answer-36ff42d1-7d91-59db-9ac4-7c3a9e11dfe7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffaee5fa-f5c5-56f1-b2fb-18822e27e724" :answer/id #uuid "ffaee5fa-f5c5-56f1-b2fb-18822e27e724" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(0.72)-Φ(-0.72)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd7d0474-7aea-50f9-ae3c-3081a543503a" :answer/id #uuid "cd7d0474-7aea-50f9-ae3c-3081a543503a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(0.98)-Φ(-0.98)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cef03ff5-191a-5d70-bca6-cb830b7fda60" :answer/id #uuid "cef03ff5-191a-5d70-bca6-cb830b7fda60" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(1.02)-Φ(-1.02)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6092f662-75ed-5f8a-9564-15a7e43b742f" :answer/id #uuid "6092f662-75ed-5f8a-9564-15a7e43b742f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(1.28)-Φ(-1.28)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36ff42d1-7d91-59db-9ac4-7c3a9e11dfe7" :answer/id #uuid "36ff42d1-7d91-59db-9ac4-7c3a9e11dfe7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(1.34)-Φ(-1.34)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9faef3f5-2412-5ae3-81dd-9f520c533b8b" :question/id #uuid "9faef3f5-2412-5ae3-81dd-9f520c533b8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-146937" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=80$ from a population where $70%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that between $60%$ and $80%$ of the sample have the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=80,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.7.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=80(0.7)=56>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=80(1-0.7)=24>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=80$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.7,\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{80}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.7,0.002\\,625).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.6\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.8).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.6\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.8) & =P(\\frac{0.6-0.7}{\\sqrt{0.002\\,625}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.8-0.7}{\\sqrt{0.002\\,625}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.95\\le Z\\le 1.95) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.95)-P(Z\\le -1.95) \\\\  & =Φ(1.95)-Φ(-1.95)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e66048ec-88b7-5ae4-8b5d-5521b6f747e3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e66048ec-88b7-5ae4-8b5d-5521b6f747e3" :answer-field/id #uuid "e66048ec-88b7-5ae4-8b5d-5521b6f747e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f918d5a6-7a95-5bf9-9d8e-e78ed7535f51" :answer-field/choices ["answer-076508d1-5160-580c-894f-c6ee9485bf2e" "answer-06171ae9-aab5-5555-8742-22098a666ee1" "answer-551a8f15-eac2-577e-8054-fb57a38b76a5" "answer-f918d5a6-7a95-5bf9-9d8e-e78ed7535f51" "answer-54ade330-5c85-51a0-9b3d-21d097f4a0f5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-076508d1-5160-580c-894f-c6ee9485bf2e" :answer/id #uuid "076508d1-5160-580c-894f-c6ee9485bf2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(0.69)-Φ(-0.69)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06171ae9-aab5-5555-8742-22098a666ee1" :answer/id #uuid "06171ae9-aab5-5555-8742-22098a666ee1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(1.19)-Φ(-1.19)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-551a8f15-eac2-577e-8054-fb57a38b76a5" :answer/id #uuid "551a8f15-eac2-577e-8054-fb57a38b76a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(1.36)-Φ(-1.36)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f918d5a6-7a95-5bf9-9d8e-e78ed7535f51" :answer/id #uuid "f918d5a6-7a95-5bf9-9d8e-e78ed7535f51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(1.95)-Φ(-1.95)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54ade330-5c85-51a0-9b3d-21d097f4a0f5" :answer/id #uuid "54ade330-5c85-51a0-9b3d-21d097f4a0f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Φ(2.26)-Φ(-2.26)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-08394bc0-c2d2-5c3c-828d-b5a2969449ba" :question/id #uuid "08394bc0-c2d2-5c3c-828d-b5a2969449ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-147070" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=96$ from a population where $36%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that less than $28%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.0537\\,$ | $\\,0.0526\\,$ | $\\,0.0516\\,$ | $\\,0.0505\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.0655\\,$ | $\\,0.0643\\,$ | $\\,0.0630\\,$ | $\\,0.0618\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0793\\,$ | $\\,0.0778\\,$ | $\\,0.0764\\,$ | $\\,0.0749\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=96,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.36.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=96(0.36)=34.56>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=96(1-0.36)=61.44>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=96$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.36,\\frac{0.36(1-0.36)}{96}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.36,0.0024).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.28).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.28) & =P(Z<\\frac{0.28-0.36}{\\sqrt{0.0024}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.63) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.63).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1.63) & =0.0516.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.28) & =Φ(-1.63) \\\\  & =0.0516.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e4643a15-31bb-5228-8c72-0d5dcdaebd79"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e4643a15-31bb-5228-8c72-0d5dcdaebd79" :answer-field/id #uuid "e4643a15-31bb-5228-8c72-0d5dcdaebd79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f2a58d8e-66ae-563e-a563-1b7fc88e1a4e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a11c1dd4-4d83-5f50-ab22-450ede146cbf" "answer-f2a58d8e-66ae-563e-a563-1b7fc88e1a4e" "answer-9fce1eb7-6882-55e9-9a78-19b8b9ec12de" "answer-a4ee7691-eaaa-5a44-8e4b-9c9a62cb1212" "answer-ddf18dde-67fe-5d37-ba3e-bc04e8232dc3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a11c1dd4-4d83-5f50-ab22-450ede146cbf" :answer/id #uuid "a11c1dd4-4d83-5f50-ab22-450ede146cbf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.0428" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2a58d8e-66ae-563e-a563-1b7fc88e1a4e" :answer/id #uuid "f2a58d8e-66ae-563e-a563-1b7fc88e1a4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.0516" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9fce1eb7-6882-55e9-9a78-19b8b9ec12de" :answer/id #uuid "9fce1eb7-6882-55e9-9a78-19b8b9ec12de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.0565" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4ee7691-eaaa-5a44-8e4b-9c9a62cb1212" :answer/id #uuid "a4ee7691-eaaa-5a44-8e4b-9c9a62cb1212" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.0615" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ddf18dde-67fe-5d37-ba3e-bc04e8232dc3" :answer/id #uuid "ddf18dde-67fe-5d37-ba3e-bc04e8232dc3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.0784" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-695f57f1-7bc3-58a6-8971-b31a35d4ca12" :activity/id #uuid "695f57f1-7bc3-58a6-8971-b31a35d4ca12" :activity/title "Point Estimates of Population Proportions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049862 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-119b64cb-4a4b-5784-891f-c5aa424cbe05" "step-e2bec5e3-fc63-5c73-984d-80b18cc96044" "step-7b7ccdb8-8a3b-5849-930e-28332d5bed17"] :activity/first-step "step-119b64cb-4a4b-5784-891f-c5aa424cbe05" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-119b64cb-4a4b-5784-891f-c5aa424cbe05" :step/id #uuid "119b64cb-4a4b-5784-891f-c5aa424cbe05" :step/content 17592186188683 :step/next "step-e2bec5e3-fc63-5c73-984d-80b18cc96044" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e2bec5e3-fc63-5c73-984d-80b18cc96044" :step/id #uuid "e2bec5e3-fc63-5c73-984d-80b18cc96044" :step/content "question-9faef3f5-2412-5ae3-81dd-9f520c533b8b" :step/next "step-7b7ccdb8-8a3b-5849-930e-28332d5bed17" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7b7ccdb8-8a3b-5849-930e-28332d5bed17" :step/id #uuid "7b7ccdb8-8a3b-5849-930e-28332d5bed17" :step/content "question-08394bc0-c2d2-5c3c-828d-b5a2969449ba" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078458 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fb5ed83f-adbe-5177-b929-40e3c3c8ee7a" "question-47fb24d6-2403-53dd-8dbf-2b7132f71263" "question-1eed18c2-ffc9-58c2-a876-148bf6a4cade"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-fb5ed83f-adbe-5177-b929-40e3c3c8ee7a" :question/id #uuid "fb5ed83f-adbe-5177-b929-40e3c3c8ee7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-14126" :question/problem "Factor $48{s}^{4}-3$ completely." :question/worked-solution "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that all of the terms have a common factor of $3.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n48{s}^{4}-3=3\\cdot 16{s}^{4}-3\\cdot 1=3(16{s}^{4}-1)\n$$\n\nThe expression in parentheses can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n3(16{s}^{4}-1)=3({(4{s}^{2})}^{2}-{1}^{2})\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n3({(4{s}^{2})}^{2}-{1}^{2})=3(4{s}^{2}+1)(4{s}^{2}-1)\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares. Therefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(4{s}^{2}+1)(4{s}^{2}-1) & =3(4{s}^{2}+1)({(2s)}^{2}-{1}^{2}) \\\\  & =3(4{s}^{2}+1)(2s+1)(2s-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-06afac61-4a09-516c-87cd-b89382f9efec"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-06afac61-4a09-516c-87cd-b89382f9efec" :answer-field/id #uuid "06afac61-4a09-516c-87cd-b89382f9efec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5e441f3c-74ea-5954-9b04-0b444ffa3de2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8b8538c7-1b01-599b-b150-a73270292e7e" "answer-6aecb389-d54f-575d-8a85-3c0beb8b5f30" "answer-5e441f3c-74ea-5954-9b04-0b444ffa3de2" "answer-924d5c59-cd65-56c5-8b49-dc5a89011fa1" "answer-61602e33-9240-55e5-ae67-6253734db54b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b8538c7-1b01-599b-b150-a73270292e7e" :answer/id #uuid "8b8538c7-1b01-599b-b150-a73270292e7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(4{s}^{2}+1)(2s+1)(2s-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6aecb389-d54f-575d-8a85-3c0beb8b5f30" :answer/id #uuid "6aecb389-d54f-575d-8a85-3c0beb8b5f30" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(4{s}^{2}+1)(3s+1)(3s-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e441f3c-74ea-5954-9b04-0b444ffa3de2" :answer/id #uuid "5e441f3c-74ea-5954-9b04-0b444ffa3de2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3(4{s}^{2}+1)(2s+1)(2s-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-924d5c59-cd65-56c5-8b49-dc5a89011fa1" :answer/id #uuid "924d5c59-cd65-56c5-8b49-dc5a89011fa1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3(4{s}^{2}+1)(s+1)(s-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61602e33-9240-55e5-ae67-6253734db54b" :answer/id #uuid "61602e33-9240-55e5-ae67-6253734db54b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3({s}^{2}+1)(2s+1)(2s-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-47fb24d6-2403-53dd-8dbf-2b7132f71263" :question/id #uuid "47fb24d6-2403-53dd-8dbf-2b7132f71263" :question/math-academy-id "q-14131" :question/problem "Factor $4{z}^{4}-324$ completely." :question/worked-solution "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that all of the terms have a common factor of $4.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n4{z}^{4}-324=4\\cdot {z}^{4}-4\\cdot 81=4({z}^{4}-81)\n$$\n\nThe expression in parentheses can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n4({z}^{4}-81)=4({({z}^{2})}^{2}-{9}^{2})\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n4({({z}^{2})}^{2}-{9}^{2})=4({z}^{2}+9)({z}^{2}-9)\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares. Therefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({z}^{2}+9)({z}^{2}-9) & =4({z}^{2}+9)({z}^{2}-{3}^{2}) \\\\  & =4({z}^{2}+9)(z+3)(z-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-87c0e667-8c54-5839-81a6-2f3012b2915d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-87c0e667-8c54-5839-81a6-2f3012b2915d" :answer-field/id #uuid "87c0e667-8c54-5839-81a6-2f3012b2915d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b4e023d2-5c4f-53c0-a51e-017f827fbd2c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-96436117-2f00-5b77-bdde-7a2e1ea0b5f8" "answer-7e8855f8-54de-5296-8668-a577edca3ea7" "answer-a967a549-543c-5830-94ce-4a491fa889e7" "answer-b4e023d2-5c4f-53c0-a51e-017f827fbd2c" "answer-6fbc1e55-9ff4-57db-a8d5-126770ed73c1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96436117-2f00-5b77-bdde-7a2e1ea0b5f8" :answer/id #uuid "96436117-2f00-5b77-bdde-7a2e1ea0b5f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "({z}^{2}+36)(z+3)(z-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e8855f8-54de-5296-8668-a577edca3ea7" :answer/id #uuid "7e8855f8-54de-5296-8668-a577edca3ea7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "({z}^{2}+9)(z+3)(z-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a967a549-543c-5830-94ce-4a491fa889e7" :answer/id #uuid "a967a549-543c-5830-94ce-4a491fa889e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4({z}^{2}+36)(z+6)(z-6)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4e023d2-5c4f-53c0-a51e-017f827fbd2c" :answer/id #uuid "b4e023d2-5c4f-53c0-a51e-017f827fbd2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4({z}^{2}+9)(z+3)(z-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6fbc1e55-9ff4-57db-a8d5-126770ed73c1" :answer/id #uuid "6fbc1e55-9ff4-57db-a8d5-126770ed73c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4({z}^{2}-6)(z+9)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1eed18c2-ffc9-58c2-a876-148bf6a4cade" :question/id #uuid "1eed18c2-ffc9-58c2-a876-148bf6a4cade" :question/math-academy-id "q-14138" :question/problem "Factor $486-6{x}^{4}$ completely." :question/worked-solution "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that all of the terms have a common factor of $6.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $6,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n486-6{x}^{4}=6\\cdot 81-6\\cdot {x}^{4}=6(81-{x}^{4})\n$$\n\nThe expression in parentheses can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n6(81-{x}^{4})=6({9}^{2}-{({x}^{2})}^{2})\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n6({9}^{2}-{({x}^{2})}^{2})=6(9+{x}^{2})(9-{x}^{2})\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares. Therefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(9+{x}^{2})(9-{x}^{2}) & =6(9+{x}^{2})({3}^{2}-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =6(9+{x}^{2})(3+x)(3-x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-51b9c843-101e-597e-98f2-3448d699942e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-51b9c843-101e-597e-98f2-3448d699942e" :answer-field/id #uuid "51b9c843-101e-597e-98f2-3448d699942e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-431be612-0a45-510a-bc8f-064d5eb3f4c6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-450499eb-16be-5929-9520-1f10e65a24ec" "answer-8977d251-8b3b-5768-a493-f8eca8cc7e33" "answer-431be612-0a45-510a-bc8f-064d5eb3f4c6" "answer-26f80dbb-9c0a-50e9-af75-fd6ae25f77d2" "answer-96da69d6-ce5d-553a-aa16-d9284a89737b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-450499eb-16be-5929-9520-1f10e65a24ec" :answer/id #uuid "450499eb-16be-5929-9520-1f10e65a24ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(9+{x}^{2})(3-{x}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8977d251-8b3b-5768-a493-f8eca8cc7e33" :answer/id #uuid "8977d251-8b3b-5768-a493-f8eca8cc7e33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6(81+{x}^{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-431be612-0a45-510a-bc8f-064d5eb3f4c6" :answer/id #uuid "431be612-0a45-510a-bc8f-064d5eb3f4c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6(9+{x}^{2})(3+x)(3-x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26f80dbb-9c0a-50e9-af75-fd6ae25f77d2" :answer/id #uuid "26f80dbb-9c0a-50e9-a